  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively point toward powder-based fabrication and in-situ alloying of refractory HEAs, consolidation near 1400°C, BCC phase formation, interstitial/oxide strengthening, cooling-dependent phase retention, and protective oxide formation. R1 is especially strong mechanistic precedent because it concerns HfNbTaTiZr and directly links oxygen above about 0.8 wt.% to substantial strengthening. R16 establishes additive manufacture with in-situ alloying of mixed refractory powders, while several records establish sintering routes and oxygen-derived strengthening. However, none describes extrusion printing of a hydride-containing binder ink, hydrogen de-binding, sequential hydride decomposition according to formation enthalpy, titanium-sponge oxygen control, or the self-healing silicide coating. Thus the literature gives relevant ingredients and direction, but the specific integrated intervention and its hydride-mediated mechanism require substantial new ideas."
